    <div class="prose">
      <h2>How I build a roadmap</h2>

      <h3>1. Start from the business goal, not the audit</h3>
      <p>
        Before crawling anything I want to know what the client is actually
        trying to achieve this year, what a lead is worth, which services carry
        margin, and where capacity exists. A recommendation that drives traffic
        to a service the client can't deliver more of is a bad recommendation,
        however good the SEO reasoning behind it.
      </p>

      <h3>2. Size the opportunity before committing to it</h3>
      <p>
        For each candidate initiative I want a rough answer to: how much
        realistic search demand exists, what position is achievable given the
        competitive set, what the expected conversion behavior is, and what it
        will cost to execute. These estimates are imprecise and I treat them as
        such &mdash; but a rough expected-value estimate beats an unordered list
        of best practices every time.
      </p>

      <h3>3. Prioritize by expected impact, not by severity label</h3>
      <p>
        Audit tools produce severity ratings. Severity is not impact. A
        "critical" issue on a page nobody lands on matters less than a moderate
        issue on the page that drives a third of inquiries. I sequence by
        expected business impact and effort, and I'm explicit about the handful
        of things I'm deliberately choosing <em>not</em> to do.
      </p>

      <h3>4. Make recommendations that a developer can implement</h3>
      <p>
        I write recommendations that specify the actual change, because I've
        spent years on the implementation side. "Improve site speed" is not a
        recommendation. Naming the render-blocking resource, the oversized
        image, or the plugin doing the damage is.
      </p>

      <h3>5. Monitor after implementation and adjust</h3>
      <p>
        Shipping is the start of the measurement window. Each initiative gets a
        defined success measure and a review point. Some bets don't pay off;
        saying so early and reallocating is more valuable than defending the
        original plan.
      </p>

      <h2>How I communicate</h2>

      <p>
        I spend a lot of my time translating. The audit finding and the
        conversation with the practice owner are two different artifacts, and
        collapsing them produces either a confused client or a developer with
        nothing actionable.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>With developers:</strong> specific, reproducible, with the
          exact element or template named.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>With writers and designers:</strong> the intent and the
          audience question being answered, not a keyword quota.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>With paid media:</strong> shared query data and landing page
          learnings. Organic and paid produce better results when they're
          comparing notes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>With clients and stakeholders:</strong> what changed, why,
          what we're doing about it, and what to expect next &mdash; without
          jargon, and without pretending to more certainty than I have.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Earlier in my career I delivered 33 live presentations of website and
        marketing assessments, which directly contributed to closing more than
        20 new builds. Explaining technical findings persuasively to
        non-technical decision-makers is a skill I built deliberately.
      </p>

      <h2>What I believe about SEO</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          Rankings are a diagnostic, not a goal. I've written publicly about why
          clients shouldn't judge performance by Googling themselves.
        </li>
        <li>
          Most sites have more to gain from fixing what already ranks than from
          publishing something new.
        </li>
        <li>
          Duplicate and templated content is a real liability, not a
          theoretical one. I've watched sites get de-ranked for it.
        </li>
        <li>
          Technical SEO is a prerequisite, not a differentiator. It earns the
          right to compete; content and relevance win.
        </li>
        <li>
          Incremental improvement usually beats a full redesign, and costs the
          client far less risk.
        </li>
      </ul>
